  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ensure respect for, promote and realize fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In January 2021, Sri Lanka raised the minimum age of admission to employment to 16 years to keep children in school longer and to raise the age of entry into the labour market, which now corresponds with the age for compulsory education. 
The draft list of hazardous occupations for children in employment was further reviewed in 2020-2021, within Government, in view of recent developments, and adopted in 2021 &#x2013; as pledged by Sri Lanka as part of its efforts during the International Year for the Elimination of Child Labour.
In 2020-21, an analysis of communication gaps in awareness of child labour and legal standards was executed by the Department of Labour, with the support of the ILO, and presented to the National Steering Committee on Child Labour. This is now guiding the Ministry of Labour&apos;s strategy during the International Year of the Elimination of Child Labour. With support from the United States, ILO had earlier provided the technical assistance to draft an expanded list of hazardous occupations &#x2013; in 2017-2018 - (for e.g. transport or use of agrochemicals; clinical or biological waste etc.) for persons under the age of 18 years in employment. 
This list was reviewed by the National Child Protection Authority and the Ministry of Labour in 2021, (following public outcry over the death of a child domestic worker) to include domestic work as a hazardous occupation for persons under 18 years of age, and is in the process of finalisation and adoption by the Cabinet of Ministers.</narrative>
   </description>
